      "message": "Most of my questions are clear now. A few scenarios and edge cases remain that I would still like to clarify:\n\n1) Blocked contacts and participant changes during a call: What happens if, during an ongoing 1:1 call or conference call, the call is forwarded to or a participant is added who is blocked by another participant (or vice versa)? Are there any differences between 1:1 calls, conference calls, and mixed scenarios where some participants are blocked and others are not? What happens if a participant is blocked or unblocked while the call is already in progress, or while another participant is being added or removed?\n\nIn particular, does the fix from patch set 3 — where blocking/removing a contact immediately ends the call and a blocked peer is sent a BYE — also cover these cases if the block/remove operation happens while SIP channel recovery or the ICE restart is already in progress?\n\n2) When and how the contact was blocked: Does it make any difference how the contact was blocked (e.g. group only, 1:1 only, both in the Swarm, and whether the contact was kicked or blocked)? Does it also make any difference whether the contact is offline before, during, or after the blocking, or alternates between being online and offline?\n\n3) Multiple devices using the same Jami account: Jami allows the same account/username to be used on multiple devices. For example:\n\nAccount A / Device 1 ↔ Account A / Device 2\n\nthen Account A / Device 3 is added as another participant.\n\nAre the individual devices treated as separate peers for the handover mechanism? Could one device of the same account perform a network handover while another device of that account remains connected? Are there any special cases when multiple devices belonging to the same account participate in the same call?\n\n4) Self-blocking / device-specific block state: What happens if an account blocks itself? Is the block state synchronized across all devices of the account, or can it be device-specific? For example, could a self-block on Device 2 prevent the conversation from being downloaded or accessed there while Device 1 remains unaffected? If so, could this have any implications for an ongoing call or SIP channel recovery?\n\n5) Simultaneous network changes on both sides: What happens if both peers detect a network change at approximately the same time and both sides start recovery? Is this explicitly handled by the current handover mechanism?\n\n6) Another network change during recovery: What happens if the network changes again while recovery is already in progress, for example Wi-Fi → LTE → Wi-Fi within the 20-second recovery window? Is the existing recovery restarted, replaced, or allowed to continue against the connection it is already recovering to?\n\n7) The old SIP channel becomes available again: After a successful handover, what happens if the old SIP channel becomes available again? The description says that a peer never moves back to a channel it has left. Is the old channel explicitly ignored/closed in this situation, and can it ever cause stale SIP/ICE state to be used again?\n\n8) Peer temporarily goes offline during recovery: What happens if the peer goes offline after recovery has started and comes back online before or after the 20-second timeout? Is the recovery strictly limited to that 20-second window, or can a newly reachable peer continue or restart the recovery?\n\n9) Network-change notification while the old channel still works: If the client explicitly reports a network change via networkInterfaceChanged(), but the existing SIP channel is still working, does the daemon immediately create a new SIP channel and restart ICE, or does it first wait until the existing channel actually fails? In other words, is the notification itself sufficient to trigger a handover, or does it only allow recovery to happen earlier if the old path becomes unusable?\n\n10) JAMS: Does the same behavior apply to Jami accounts managed/provisioned through the Jami Account Management Server (JAMS), or are there any JAMS-specific aspects that need to be taken into account for SIP channel recovery?\n\n11) Conferences and participant changes: Since conference calls and subcalls keep their previous behavior, does that also mean that network handover/recovery for conferences is intentionally out of scope? In particular, are scenarios such as adding/removing participants, forwarding a call to a blocked participant, or having mixed blocked/unblocked participants during a conference expected to continue using the previous behavior?",
